# §3. What this settles and what it deliberately does not

**Settled — the invariance lemma (Route C, modified form).** The λ-map is a
deterministic bijection of sample space, so the *model family* — laws over
pin-preserving degree-4 polynomials — is gauge-invariant; only the induced law on
\((s,a,w,z,c_{\cdot\cdot})\) carries content. There is nothing to gauge-fix.
GP-AUD-020 §8 condition 3 asked for a proof that the modes "cannot change the
measured adjacency observable": the precise true statement is that they cannot
change it **except through the jet law**, and the jet law is exactly what Route A
derives. The objection is upheld and localized — the missing object is the
finite-r law of four jets the program already samples, not a new compensation
layer.

**Not settled — and this keeps GP-ERR-024's demotion fully in force:**
(a) the actual finite-r values. Mean shifts are O(r²) —
\(E[w]=r^2/4\) is derived; the analogous \(E[\delta s],E[\delta a],E[\delta z]\)
and the **O(r) covariance corrections** of the cubic-jet law (which are *not*
expressible as fixed λ's — they are random-coefficient corrections at the same
order as \(r\tilde P_4\)) remain underived. Both arms of GP-DATA-020 omitted them
identically, so the Q-NULL *difference* is clean while neither arm's absolute
value is — my AUD-008 §5 philosophy-(A)/(B) call, now sharpened rather than
revised. (b) Route A stands, with its target clarified: derive the O(r)/O(r²)
corrections to the \((s,a,w,z)\) conditional law plus the finite-r \(c\)-jet law;
no fourth-order gauge coefficients exist beyond that.

