# §4. Answers to GP-PRE-025, and frozen predictions before it runs

**The requested non-arbitrary scale:** \(\lambda_{\text{phys}}=O(r)\). Worked
anchor: \(E[w]=r^2/4\ \Leftrightarrow\ \lambda_3=r/8\approx0.003\) at r=0.025.
The frozen grid \(|\lambda|\in\{1,2,4\}\) therefore probes perturbations
**~300–1300×** physical. Two consequences worth adopting before execution:

- **Report derivatives, not just verdicts.** With CRN the per-mode derivative
  \(\partial\log(\text{weighted}/r^3)/\partial\lambda_i\) is cleanly estimable
  from the ±1, ±2, ±4 rows; multiplying by \(\lambda_{\text{phys}}=O(r)\) converts
  the 31-gauge design into a direct estimate of the *physical* finite-r
  sensitivity — no rerun needed once Route A supplies the coefficients.
- **A G2 at \(|\lambda|\ge1\) would not indict the physical model** — it would
  measure sensitivity to unphysically large mis-centerings. G1/G2 remain the
  right frozen readings for the *family*; the physical claim routes through the
  derivative.

**Frozen predictions (Rule 7), committed before execution:**

- **P1:** G1 fires — every gauge with ≥20 escapes within a factor 4 of baseline;
  quantitatively, max deviation of weighted/r³ ≤ ~2× baseline at every rung, no
  value above 10⁻⁴, no outside-screen escape. Basis: an s-shift of
  \(2r\lambda\le0.2\) against a failure layer of width ~4 in s, with the Gaussian
  factor varying on s-scale \(\sqrt2/r\gg1\).
- **P2:** the λ₂ (z-shift) one-mode derivative is the **smallest** of the four at
  every rung — z enters neither \(m_{22,S}\) nor the endpoint Hessians, only the
  cubic transverse forcing.
- **P3 (linearity):** the (+1,+1,+1,+1) joint response equals the sum of the four
  +1 one-mode responses within 30%, per rung where ≥20 escapes. **If P3 fails, my
  small-shift framing is wrong at their grid scale and §4's derivative
